# REVIEWED BY A SECOND MODEL, NOT BY A NATIVE SPEAKER. The difference matters
# and is the reason this block still exists.
#
# Every msgstr below was produced by machine translation and has since been
# read end to end, in context and in batches grouped by the program each string
# comes from, by a different model family from the one that wrote it
# (qwen3.8-max). 38 changes were applied to this catalogue.
#
# What that review IS: a careful second pass by something that does not share
# the first pass's blind spots, held to a written brief -- change what is wrong,
# do not restyle what is merely different -- with every proposed change put
# through mechanical guards (tools/i18n-review.py) that reject a translation
# which loses a $VAR, drops a URL, translates a product name, or breaks an
# accepted-answer list. 0 were rejected. 0 untouched strings drifted.
#
# What it is NOT: a native speaker of Italian reading this for release. Nobody
# who speaks Italian as a first language has read this file. If that ever
# happens, this block is what should be replaced, and the five categories below
# are still where to start -- they are the strings that cost money when they
# are wrong, and a second machine pass does not retire that risk, only reduce
# it:
#
#   * everything from dagric-migrate. It touches the owner's Windows
#     partition, and the product rests on "Windows is only ever READ".
#     Negation and modality are where machine translation fails, and this
#     file is made of them. The French catalogue had exactly this defect.
#   * everything from dagric-usb-protect. A translation that loses "plug in
#     everything you normally use before continuing" produces a machine its
#     owner cannot type on.
#   * the Secure Boot notice in dagric-drivers. Getting it wrong means a
#     black screen at the next boot.
#   * the licence paragraphs in dagric-get-steam and dagric-get-resolve, and
#     the consent wording in dagric-vm. Sold in the EU: this is liability,
#     not polish.
#   * "y yes". Translating the [y/N] prompt without translating the accepted
#     answer means the owner types their own language's letter and nothing
#     happens. This catalogue gets it right; keep it that way.
#
# The [y/N] PROMPT INDICATOR is deliberately NOT localised to show the English
# letter as well. The reviewer proposed that in every language, 38 times in
# total; a three-model panel (qwen3.8-max, glm-5.2, deepseek-v4-pro) rejected it
# unanimously, including the model that proposed it. The accepted-answer list
# still takes "y" everywhere -- only the display was left alone.
#
#
# BACK-TRANSLATION QA, 2026-08-04. Every string in this catalogue was rendered
# back into English by deepseek-v4-pro, which was shown the Italian and NOTHING
# else — no English source, no product name, no description of what the program
# does. A model that cannot see the original cannot agree with it, which is the
# point: a meaning inversion survives a review that pattern-matches two texts
# into agreement, and cannot survive a round trip.
#
# glm-5.2 then compared each round trip against the real English and marked it
# SAME, DRIFT or INVERSION. Three model families, three jobs, none of them
# checking work it produced.
#
# Result for Italian: 24 of 438 flagged, 0 of them as INVERSION.
# Across all five catalogues: 3 inversions raised out of 2,189 strings, and all
# three were put to a three-model panel with the source, the translation and the
# back-translation pasted in. Unanimous REFUTED at high confidence: Spanish
# "Windows solo se LEE" is the pasiva refleja and means "Windows is only read",
# not "Windows reads"; Portuguese "rode antes X" is an adverb meaning run X
# first, not run something before X. Both were literal-back-translation
# artifacts. ZERO genuine inversions remain.
#
# That is the strongest check available short of a native speaker, and it is
# still not one. The block above stands.
#
# A string that should ship in English until it has been checked can be
# marked "#, fuzzy": msgfmt drops fuzzy messages and the English source is
# shown instead. Never build with --use-fuzzy.
# ###########################################################################
#
